  "updated": "2026-08-18T07:06:56.974619+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nThe lingering showers and storms around now will gradually\ndecrease by sunrise this morning. Another round of storms is \nforecast this afternoon and evening which looks similar to the \nsetup we had last evening. \n\nAs a vort max pivots through Montana midday today storms will \nkick off of the mountains of south-central Montana mid-to late \nafternoon just as that area of broad scale ascent moves into the \nregion. Storms should be able to initiate fairly easily in this \nregion of CVA due to the enhancement of the mountains as well as \nthe minimal CIN due to sufficient daytime heating. CAPE values \nwill be very similar to what we saw Monday with widespread \n500-1000 J/Kg. As evident from last night's storms, this will be \nplenty to sustain strong storms. Mid-level lapse rates were a \ntouch under forecast yesterday, so taking that into account, I do \nexpect good values in excess of 8 C/Km across the CWA. Moisture \ncontent also should not change much going into this evening. The \nthreats today will be the same as yesterday due to the storms \nbeing decently high based resulting in a downburst / gusty wind \nthreat. The 0-6 Km shear was also under forecast for Monday due in\nlarge part to stronger 700-500mb winds helping to tilt the \nstorms. While the winds through that layer will not be as strong \nas yesterday, there will still be widespread 30-40kts of sheer \nwhich will be plenty to sustain supercells. The main limiting \nfactor in terms of hail development is the lack of CAPE and how \nskinny the profile is. That being said, the shear in the hail \ngrowth zone is very supportive of tilted storms and hydrometer \nsize sorting. This was exactly my thinking when writing this \ndiscussion 24 hours ago and it appears that a couple storms were \nable to support golf ball sized hail. This lends itself to the \ntheory that strong shear is more important for hail development \nthan large amounts of CAPE. Whatever the true reason is, the \nanalog from Monday to Tuesday being very similar leads me to \nbelieve that there could be another considerable severe storm this\nevening wherever it is able to take advantage of the maximum of \nall these ingredients. \n\nWednesday will be a transition day as the parent upper level low \nmoves off to the east. Showers will probably be hanging around the\narea Wednesday morning and quickly diminish / move east by midday.\nBy Wednesday night a ridge will be in place over the \nintermountain region which will keep most of the CWA warm and dry.\nAs weak vort maxes ride along the ridge mid to late week, there \ncould be widely isolated showers in the eastern 2/3 of the CWA any\ngiven day. This pattern will continue into the weekend as temps \nslowly increase each day. There is still a difference between the \nGFS and ECMWF ensembles in terms of highs for this weekend as the \nGFS wants to have us flirt with triple digits again Saturday \nwhereas the ECMWF keeps highs in the low 90s.\n\nThe ridge should not stick around too long though as models hint\nat it only lasting into the weekend. The potential for a trough to\nmove off of the BC coast and track just north of the \ninternational border is possible. The timing of this feature still\nvaries greatly between the start and the end of the weekend so no\nclear forecast can be made at this time. For anyone who is \nlooking forward to the next season, this seems to have a hint of \nan Autumn flavor to it in terms of location and progression. WMR",
